    "answer": "I found and fixed the bug in `ranges/max-satisfying.js:18`. The comparison was backwards: `maxSV.compare(v) === 1` replaced the current result whenever it was *greater* than `v`, so the function returned the lowest matching version instead of the highest.\n\nThe fix is one character, changing `=== 1` to `=== -1`, so the result is now replaced only when `v` is higher:\n\n```js\nif (!max || maxSV.compare(v) === -1) {\n```\n\n**Check:** I ran the function directly with Node:\n- `['1.2.3','1.2.4','1.3.0']` with `^1.2.0` now returns `1.3.0`, the case from the report.\n- The same list in reverse order also returns `1.3.0`.\n- `['2.0.0']` with `^1.2.0` returns `null`, as it should.\n\nI did not run the test suite (`npm test`) because dependencies aren't installed. I didn't change any tests."
