    "answer": "I fixed it. The bug was a reversed comparison in `ranges/max-satisfying.js`, at the line `if (!max || maxSV.compare(v) === 1)`.\n\n`maxSV.compare(v) === 1` is true when the current max is **greater** than `v`. So each time a satisfying version was *smaller*, it became the new \"max\", which made the function return the minimum. I changed it to `=== -1`, so the max is only replaced by a higher version. That's the whole change: one character on one line, and no tests were touched.\n\n**Checked:** I ran the code directly with Node:\n- `maxSatisfying(['1.2.3','1.2.4','1.3.0'], '^1.2.0')` now returns `1.3.0`.\n- With the list in reverse order it also returns `1.3.0`.\n- `minSatisfying` still returns `1.2.3`.\n\n**Not checked:** I didn't run `npm test`, because the dependencies (`tap`) aren't installed."
